% =====================================================
\section*{Abstract}

We study the problem of maximizing expected gain from a return-predicting signal net of the impact cost of one's own trades, under a propagator impact model with a general symmetric positive-definite kernel. Existing treatments express the adapted optimal trading rate as the solution of an integral or stochastic Volterra equation. We give a closed-form bulk expression in terms of the driving signal using a Wiener-Hopf factorization of the impact cost operator. For the empirically supported power-law kernel $G(t)=t^{-\beta}$ with $\beta\in(0,1)$, the factors are fractional integrals, and the optimal trading policy simplifies to a composition of fractional derivatives of order $(1-\beta)/2$ applied to the signal and its forecasts. On a finite horizon the same recipe produces an optimal policy that converges to the whole-line formula in the interior at rate $O(d(t)^{-(1-\beta)/2})$, where $d(t)$ is the distance from $t$ to the nearest boundary.

% =====================================================
\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The gain--cost problem}

Institutional trading of large orders --- index rebalances, algorithmic strategies against short-horizon forecasts, hedges against derivative books --- routinely moves quantities that are large multiples of top-of-book depth. Empirical studies of trade-and-quote data \citep{LilloFarmerMantegna2003,BouchaudGefenPottersWyart2004,Gatheral2010,JusselinRosenbaum2020} establish that each child order pushes the mid-price against the trader's direction and that the resulting \emph{transient impact} is well described by a translation-invariant propagator kernel $G(t-s)$ that decays with lag: the price at time $t$ receives a contribution $\int_{s\le t}G(t-s)\,u_s\,ds$ from the trader's own past rate $u$. The cost of a large parent order therefore depends on the whole path of its execution schedule, not only on its instantaneous rate.

When the trader holds a return-predicting signal $\alpha_t$ --- a forecast of price change conditional on time-$t$ information --- the gain from trading in the signal's direction competes with the impact cost of moving into the position. Under the propagator model of Bouchaud, Gefen, Potters and Wyart \citeyearpar{BouchaudGefenPottersWyart2004}, extended by Gatheral \citeyearpar{Gatheral2010}, the expected P\&L of an adapted trading rate $u\in L^2_{\adap}$ is a linear gain against a quadratic cost, and the \emph{gain--cost problem} is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gaincost}
\max_{u\in L^2_{\adap}}\ \E\!\int u_t\,\alpha_t\,dt \;-\; \tfrac{\gamma}{2}\,\E\!\iint G(|t-v|)\, u_t\, u_v\,dt\,dv,
\end{equation}
with $\gamma>0$ a cost-aversion coupling. Write $C$ for the symmetric convolution $(Cu)(t) = \int G(|t-v|)u_v\,dv$, positive-definite ($\hat C(\xi)\ge 0$) to rule out static round-trip arbitrage. Two kernels run through the paper: the empirically supported power law $G(t) = |t|^{-\beta}$, $\beta\in(0,1)$ estimated at $0.2$--$0.6$ across markets and asset classes \citep{LilloFarmerMantegna2003,BouchaudGefenPottersWyart2004,Gatheral2010,JusselinRosenbaum2020}, with symbol $\hat C(\xi) = c_\beta|\xi|^{\beta-1}$ and $c_\beta = 2\Gamma(1-\beta)\sin(\pi\beta/2)$; and the tractable exponential $G(t) = e^{-\kappa|t|}$, $\kappa>0$, with symbol $2\kappa/(\kappa^2+\xi^2)$. The power-law symbol has a low-frequency singularity and $C$ is unbounded on $L^2(\R)$; \S4 gives the technical domain fix required for the pure power-law setting. The exponential kernel and the finite-horizon truncation to $[0,T]$ are bounded on $L^2$ without further regularization.

\subsection{Analogy with Markowitz portfolio choice}

Problem \eqref{eq:gaincost} has the same convex-quadratic structure as the mean--variance portfolio problem \citep{Markowitz1952,Merton1972},
\begin{equation}\label{eq:markowitz}
\max_{w\in\R^N}\ w^\top\mu \;-\; \tfrac{1}{2}\lambda\, w^\top\Sigma w,
\end{equation}
under the correspondence
\[
(w,\,\mu,\,\Sigma,\,\lambda) \;\longleftrightarrow\; (u,\,\alpha,\,C,\,\gamma).
\]
The Markowitz optimum is $w^\star = \lambda^{-1}\Sigma^{-1}\mu$ with value $\tfrac{1}{2\lambda}\mu^\top\Sigma^{-1}\mu$ (the Mahalanobis norm of expected return, squared). The direct analog $u^\star = \gamma^{-1}C^{-1}\alpha$ would be the optimum of the trading problem before imposing adaptedness and accounting for the non-locality of the Hessian. Two features of the temporal problem block the direct analog:

\emph{(i) Non-locality of the Hessian.} $C$ is a temporal convolution against a symmetric kernel, so $(Cu)(t)$ depends on $u_v$ for $v$ both before and after $t$.

\emph{(ii) Adaptedness of the feasible set.} The trading rate $u_t$ at time $t$ must be $\F_t$-measurable; computing $(C^{-1}\alpha)_t$ requires future values of $\alpha$ which the trader does not have.

\subsection{Prior treatments}

Existing treatments of problem \eqref{eq:gaincost} establish existence, uniqueness, and structural characterizations of $u^\star$.

\emph{Deterministic constant-signal case.} Almgren and Chriss \citeyearpar{AlmgrenChriss2001} treat quadratic cost with the exponential kernel and derive closed-form schedules by state augmentation. Obizhaeva and Wang \citeyearpar{ObizhaevaWang2013} solve the same problem in a limit-order-book resilience framework. For the power-law kernel, Gatheral, Schied and Slynko \citeyearpar{GatheralSchiedSlynko2012} give the optimal deterministic schedule as an eigenfunction of a Fredholm integral equation, exhibiting the S\"ohngen--Tricomi endpoint weights $(t(T-t))^{(\beta-1)/2}$. In these papers the ``signal'' is a Lagrange multiplier fixed by the terminal-inventory constraint; the trader has no additional information.

\emph{Stochastic-signal case, exponential kernel.} Lehalle and Neuman \citeyearpar{LehalleNeuman2019} solve the Almgren--Chriss problem with a general adapted signal and linear temporary impact, obtaining $u^\star$ as a conditional expectation of the future signal --- the temporary-impact cost operator is diagonal, so the projected inverse reduces to the projection itself. Neuman and Vo\ss{} \citeyearpar{NeumanVoss2022} extend the analysis to the exponential resilience kernel, using the Markov property to reduce the problem to a finite-dimensional linear-quadratic control with Riccati feedback --- available via state augmentation specific to the exponential kernel and not to the empirically supported power-law case. Cartea, Jaimungal and Penalva \citeyearpar{CarteaJaimungalPenalva2015} develop the broader algorithmic-trading framework.

\emph{Stochastic-signal case, general kernel.} For a general propagator kernel with a stochastic signal, Abi Jaber and Neuman \citeyearpar{AbiJaberNeuman2025} prove existence and uniqueness of $u^\star$ and characterize it as the unique solution of a linear stochastic Volterra equation of the second kind: a fixed-point equation involving the kernel and the signal, from which closed forms are recovered only after kernel-specific reductions. The framework extends to matrix-valued cross-impact \citep{AbiJaberNeumanTuschmann2024} and constrained trading including battery-storage applications \citep{AbiJaberDeCarvalhoPham2024}, inheriting the same Volterra-equation form.

\emph{Stochastic-signal case, power-law kernel.} Forde, S\'anchez-Betancourt and Smith \citeyearpar{FordeSanchezSmith2022} treat Gaussian signals on $[0,T]$ using the finite-interval factorization $G_T = T\,T^*$ with $T$ a Volterra operator of Riemann--Liouville type \citep[Ex.~6.2,~9.2]{PorterStirling1990}; the optimal rate is the integral of the signal against the resolvent of $T$, inverted via the Chakrabarti--George Abel formula \citep{ChakrabartiGeorge1994}. The representation is horizon-tied and gives no bulk formula in terms of the signal path.

\emph{Deterministic factorization theory.} Wiener and Hopf \citeyearpar{WienerHopf1931} introduced the factorization of scalar Fourier symbols on a half-line as the basis for solving convolution integral equations. Krein \citeyearpar{Krein1962} developed the corresponding half-line integral-equation theory, and Wiener \citeyearpar{Wiener1949} applied the factorization to stationary linear prediction. Gohberg and Krein \citeyearpar{GohbergKrein1970} established the finite-interval factorization $I+K = (I+L^*)(I+L)$ for positive kernels via Volterra triangularization, and Arveson \citeyearpar{Arveson1975} generalized the picture to positive operators on Hilbert spaces equipped with a continuous nest of projections.

Across these strands the adapted optimal rate under a stochastic signal and a power-law kernel is characterized implicitly, through fixed-point Volterra equations or horizon-tied resolvent representations. No bulk closed form in terms of the signal path has been given, and the fractional-calculus structure of the problem is left buried in the resolvent. The factorization identity below closes both gaps.

\subsection{Contribution}

We give a closed formula for the adapted optimal trading rate under a general symmetric positive-definite propagator kernel, and show that for the power-law kernel it reduces in the interior of the trading horizon to a fractional derivative of the trader's forecast curve.

\paragraph{Closed formula.} For any factorization $C = C_-\,C_+$ of the cost operator into a causal Volterra factor $C_+$ and its adjoint $C_-$, the adapted first-order condition of \eqref{eq:gaincost} admits the solution
\begin{equation}\label{eq:closedform-intro}
u^\star \;=\; \gamma^{-1}\, C_+^{-1}\, P_+\, C_-^{-1}\,\alpha,
\end{equation}
via the projected-inverse identity from nest-algebra theory \citep{Arveson1975},
\begin{equation}\label{eq:pi-identity-intro}
(P_+\, C\, P_+)^{-1} \;=\; C_+^{-1}\, P_+\, C_-^{-1}
\end{equation}
on the adapted subspace $L^2_{\adap}$. The factorization is Wiener--Hopf on the whole line and Gohberg--Krein on a finite interval, and \eqref{eq:closedform-intro} closes the problem in both cases.

The three composed operators act on the signal: the anticausal square-root inverse $C_-^{-1}$ applied to $\alpha$ produces an $\F_s$-measurable ``whitened'' process at each $s$ once future signal values are replaced by their conditional expectations (the \emph{forecast curve} $\bar\alpha(t,s) = \E_t[\alpha_s]$); the projection $P_+$ enforces adaptedness; the causal square-root inverse $C_+^{-1}$ colors the whitened process back to obtain the trading rate. The three-step architecture --- anticausal whitening, adapted projection, causal coloring --- is the temporal analog of the Wiener--Kolmogorov prediction filter for stationary processes \citep{Wiener1949}, with $C_+$ in place of the process spectral square root.

\paragraph{Power-law kernel: bulk formula.} For $G(t) = |t|^{-\beta}$ the whole-line factors are the Liouville (Weyl) fractional integrals $C_\pm = c_\beta^{1/2}\,I_\pm^\nu$ with $\nu = (1-\beta)/2$, and \eqref{eq:closedform-intro} reduces to a fractional derivative applied to the forecast curve:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:bulk-intro}
u^\star_t \;=\; \gamma^{-1}\,c_\beta^{-1}\,(D_+^\nu\,\zeta)(t), \qquad \zeta_s \;=\; \bigl(D_-^\nu\,\bar\alpha(s,\cdot)\bigr)(s).
\end{equation}
The optimal rate at time $t$ is the causal Marchaud fractional derivative, of order $\nu = (1-\beta)/2$, of the ``whitened signal'' $\zeta_s$; and $\zeta_s$ is the anticausal Marchaud fractional derivative of the same order applied to the forecast curve $\bar\alpha(s,\cdot)$ and evaluated at the diagonal. Without the adapted projection the composition $D_+^\nu\,D_-^\nu$ would collapse (in the frequency domain) to the symmetric Riesz derivative of order $2\nu = 1-\beta$; the projection $P_+$ separates the two half-order pieces and prevents that collapse, so the operator $D_+^\nu\,P_+\,D_-^\nu$ is genuinely a composition of one-sided derivatives of order $\nu$ rather than a single fractional derivative. The empirical range $\beta\in(0.2,0.6)$ places each half-order in $(0.2, 0.4)$.


% =====================================================
\section{The Gain--Cost Problem in Operator Form}

\subsection{Setup}

Fix a filtered probability space $(\Omega, \F, (\F_t)_{t\in\R}, \PP)$ satisfying the usual conditions. The signal $\alpha$ is a mean-zero, progressively measurable process with stationary spectral density $S_\alpha(\xi)$ satisfying $\int(1+|\xi|^{2(1-\beta)+\epsilon})\,S_\alpha(\xi)\,d\xi<\infty$ for some $\epsilon>0$. Let $L^2 = L^2(\Omega\times\R)$ with reference measure $\PP\otimes dt$, and let $L^2_{\adap}\subset L^2$ denote the closed subspace of adapted processes: those $u$ with $u_t\in\F_t$ for a.e.\ $t$. Denote by $P_+$ the $L^2$-orthogonal projection onto $L^2_{\adap}$; pointwise,
\[
(P_+ X)_s \;=\; \E_s[X_s],
\]
which coincides with the optional projection restricted to $L^2$. Our Fourier convention is $\hat f(\xi) = \int e^{i\xi t}f(t)\,dt$; a causal function (support in $t\ge 0$) has Fourier transform analytic in the upper half-plane.

The cost operator $C$ is the deterministic convolution against a symmetric kernel $G$,
\[
(Cu)(t) \;=\; \int G(|t-v|)\, u_v\, dv, \qquad \hat C(\xi)\ge 0 \text{ on } \xi\ne 0,
\]
acting on $L^2$ diagonally in $\omega$. Positive-definiteness of $\hat C$ ensures strict convexity of the quadratic penalty in \eqref{eq:gaincost} on $L^2_{\adap}$. For the pure power-law kernel $C$ is unbounded on $L^2(\R)$ and is interpreted between homogeneous Sobolev spaces $C\colon\Ldot{-\nu}\to\Ldot{\nu}$ with $\nu=(1-\beta)/2$; the technical domain fix is developed in \S4. On $[0,T]$ the kernel is weakly singular and $G_T$ is bounded on $L^2([0,T])$ without further regularization.

The \emph{forecast curve} at time $t$ is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:forecast}
\bar\alpha(t,s) \;=\; \begin{cases}\alpha_s, & s\le t,\\ \E_t[\alpha_s], & s>t.\end{cases}
\end{equation}

\subsection{The adapted first-order condition}

Taking the G\^ateaux derivative of the objective in \eqref{eq:gaincost} against adapted variations $\delta u\in L^2_{\adap}$ and using $\E[u\,\E_t[\cdot]] = \E[u\cdot]$ for adapted $u$ gives the \emph{adapted first-order condition}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:foc}
\gamma\,\E_t\!\bigl[(C u^\star)(t)\bigr] \;=\; \alpha_t \quad\text{for a.e.\ } t.
\end{equation}
Equivalently in operator form: $\gamma\, P_+ C P_+\, u^\star = \alpha$ with $u^\star, \alpha \in L^2_{\adap}$.

The Hessian $\gamma\, P_+ C P_+$ is a symmetric strictly positive operator on $L^2_{\adap}$ (strict convexity follows from $\hat C>0$ on $\xi\ne 0$ via Plancherel applied to the extension by zero of any adapted $u$). It admits a bounded two-sided inverse, and \eqref{eq:foc} has a unique solution
\begin{equation}\label{eq:foc-op}
u^\star \;=\; \gamma^{-1}\,(P_+\, C\, P_+)^{-1}\,\alpha.
\end{equation}
The remainder of the paper computes the projected inverse in closed form.

% =====================================================
\section{Factorization of the Cost Operator}

The strategy is to split $C$ as a causal-anticausal product and reduce the projected inverse to a three-step alternating operator. This section states the factorization in both settings --- whole line and finite interval --- and proves the projected-inverse identity that underlies the closed-form solution.

\subsection{Whole line: Wiener--Hopf factorization}

\begin{proposition}[Wiener--Hopf factorization]
Let $\hat C(\xi)\ge 0$ with $\log\hat C(\xi)/(1+\xi^2)\in L^1(\R)$. Then $\hat C$ admits a factorization
\begin{equation}\label{eq:wh}
\hat C(\xi) \;=\; \hat C_-(\xi)\,\hat C_+(\xi),
\end{equation}
with $\hat C_+$ analytic and non-vanishing in the closed upper half-plane, $\hat C_-$ analytic in the closed lower half-plane, and $\overline{\hat C_+(\xi)} = \hat C_-(\xi)$; equivalently $C_+^* = C_-$. In the time domain, $C_+$ has kernel supported on $\{s\le t\}$ (causal Volterra) and $C_-$ on $\{s\ge t\}$ (anticausal). Under the normalization $\overline{\hat C_+}=\hat C_-$ the factorization is unique up to a unimodular multiplicative constant.
\end{proposition}

Existence is classical \citep{WienerHopf1931,Krein1962}. Kernel-specific factors are worked out in \S4 (power-law) and \S5.1 (exponential).

\subsection{Finite interval: Gohberg--Krein factorization}

On $[0,T]$ translation invariance is lost, Fourier factorization does not apply, and the appropriate analog is the Volterra-triangular factorization of Gohberg and Krein \citeyearpar{GohbergKrein1970}, abstractly the Arveson outer factorization on a continuous nest \citep{Arveson1975}.

\begin{proposition}[Gohberg--Krein factorization]
Let $G_T$ be the restriction of $C$ to $L^2([0,T])$, viewed as an integral operator with weakly singular kernel $G(|t-v|)\mathbf{1}_{[0,T]^2}$, and suppose $G_T$ is strictly positive on $L^2([0,T])$ (injective with $\langle G_T f,f\rangle>0$ for $f\ne0$, guaranteed by $\hat C>0$ on $\xi\ne0$ via Plancherel on the extension by zero). Then $G_T$ admits a causal--anticausal factorization
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gt-factor}
G_T \;=\; C_-\, C_+,
\end{equation}
with $C_+$ a Volterra operator whose kernel is supported on $\{s\le t\}\cap[0,T]^2$ (causal) and $C_- = C_+^*$ its adjoint (kernel on $\{s\ge t\}$). The causal factor $C_+$ is unique up to a left unitary commuting with the nest $\{P_{[0,t]}\}$ (a diagonal phase). For the power-law kernel $G_T$ is compact with unbounded inverse, so the factors act between weighted Sobolev spaces rather than on $L^2$ (\S4.1).
\end{proposition}

The classical Gohberg--Krein outer factor $T$ realizes the Arveson outer factorization of $G_T$ with respect to the continuous nest $\{P_{[0,t]}\}_{t\in[0,T]}$ as $G_T = T\,T^*$ with $T$ causal \citep[Thm~4.4.2]{Arveson1975}\citep[Ex.~6.2/9.2]{PorterStirling1990}. This places the causal factor on the \emph{left}; the adapted projected inverse of Lemma~1 (below) instead needs the causal factor on the right, obtained by the time reflection $C_+ = R\,T^*R$, $(Rf)(t)=f(T-t)$, since $R\,G_T R = G_T$ gives $C_-C_+ = R\,(TT^*)\,R = G_T$. The explicit power-law factors are given in \S4.

\subsection{The adapted projected inverse}

\begin{lemma}[Adapted inverse via causal-anticausal factorization]
Let $\Hilb$ be either $L^2(\R)$ or $L^2([0,T])$, tensored with $L^2(\Omega)$ against the filtered measure. Let $C = C_-\, C_+$ be a factorization of a symmetric strictly positive operator on $\Hilb$ into a causal Volterra factor $C_+$ (kernel supported on $\{s\le t\}$) and its adjoint $C_- = C_+^*$. Let $P_+$ be the $L^2$-orthogonal projection onto adapted processes. Then on the adapted subspace,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:pi-identity}
(P_+\, C\, P_+)^{-1} \;=\; C_+^{-1}\, P_+\, C_-^{-1}.
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof (sketch)]
Causality of $C_+$ (kernel on $\{s\le t\}$) gives $P_+^\perp C_+ P_+ = 0$, so $C_+^{-1}$ preserves the adapted subspace; its adjoint identity is $P_+ C_- P_+^\perp = 0$, so $C_-$ preserves the anticausal subspace. Splitting $C_-^{-1}u$ into adapted and anticausal parts and using the second identity to kill the anticausal contribution, one checks that for adapted $u$, $P_+ C_-^{-1} P_+ C_- u = P_+ u = u$. Composing with $C_+^{-1}$ on the left and $P_+ C P_+$ on the right returns $u$. Only causality of $C_+$ and self-adjointness of $C$ enter, so the identity covers the whole-line Wiener--Hopf and the finite-interval Gohberg--Krein/Arveson factorizations. Full details in the appendix.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Closed-form optimal trading rate}

Combining \eqref{eq:foc-op} and \eqref{eq:pi-identity}:

\begin{theorem}[Closed-form optimal rate]
Under the hypotheses of \S2.1, the unique adapted solution of \eqref{eq:foc} is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:closedform}
u^\star \;=\; \gamma^{-1}\, C_+^{-1}\, P_+\, C_-^{-1}\, \alpha,
\end{equation}
understood in either setting: on the whole line with $C_\pm$ the Wiener--Hopf factors, or on $[0,T]$ with $C_+ = R\,T^*R$ the reflected (terminal-anchored) causal Gohberg--Krein factor of $G_T$ and $C_- = C_+^*$. When linear position constraints are imposed, $\alpha$ is replaced by an effective signal $\alpha^{\eff} = \alpha + \sum_k\mu_k\psi_k$ adjoining one multiplier per constraint: a finite family for expectation constraints such as $\E[X_T]=0$, and a process-valued multiplier $\mu(\cdot)$ for the pathwise liquidation constraint $X_T=0$ a.s.\ \citep{FordeSanchezSmith2022}.
\end{theorem}

The acausal factor $C_-^{-1}$ applied to the adapted signal $\alpha$ at time $s$ requires the future path $\{\alpha_r\}_{r\ge s}$, which is not in $\F_s$. The adapted projection replaces this future by its conditional expectation, and commutation of the deterministic operator $C_-^{-1}$ with conditional expectation on the argument variable \citep[Prop.~2.6.13]{HytonenVanNeervenVeraarWeis2016} gives
\begin{equation}\label{eq:proj-cma}
\bigl(P_+\, C_-^{-1}\,\alpha\bigr)_s \;=\; \bigl(C_-^{-1}\,\bar\alpha(s,\cdot)\bigr)(s).
\end{equation}
The optimal rate is therefore
\begin{equation}\label{eq:closedform-forecast}
u^\star_t \;=\; \gamma^{-1}\,C_+^{-1}\,\zeta\,\bigl|_{t}, \qquad \zeta_s \;=\; \bigl(C_-^{-1}\,\bar\alpha(s,\cdot)\bigr)(s).
\end{equation}
The forecast curve enters only through the inner $C_-^{-1}$ step; $C_+^{-1}$ then samples the adapted process $\zeta$ over $s\le t$, producing an $\F_t$-measurable rate.

% =====================================================
\section{Power-Law Kernel}

For $G(t) = |t|^{-\beta}$ with $\beta\in(0,1)$ and $\nu = (1-\beta)/2$, the Wiener--Hopf and Gohberg--Krein factors are explicit fractional integrals, and Theorem~1 reduces to a fractional derivative of the forecast curve on the whole line and to a boundary-deformed fractional derivative on $[0,T]$.

\subsection{Fractional-integral factors}

\emph{Whole-line factors.} The symbol $\hat C(\xi) = c_\beta|\xi|^{\beta-1} = c_\beta(i\xi)^{-\nu}(-i\xi)^{-\nu}$ (standard branches) factors as $\hat C_+(\xi) = c_\beta^{1/2}(-i\xi)^{-\nu}$ (analytic in the upper half-plane) and $\hat C_-(\xi) = c_\beta^{1/2}(i\xi)^{-\nu}$. In the time domain, $C_\pm$ are the causal and anticausal Riemann--Liouville fractional integrals of order $\nu$:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:marchaud-wh}
C_+ \;=\; c_\beta^{1/2}\,I_+^\nu, \quad (I_+^\nu f)(t) = \tfrac{1}{\Gamma(\nu)}\!\int_{-\infty}^{t}(t-s)^{\nu-1}f(s)\,ds; \qquad C_- \;=\; c_\beta^{1/2}\,I_-^\nu.
\end{equation}
Inverses are the Marchaud fractional derivatives $C_\pm^{-1} = c_\beta^{-1/2}\,D_\pm^\nu$ \citep[\S5.4]{SamkoKilbasMarichev1993}.

\emph{Boundedness caveat.} The pure power-law symbol satisfies the log-integrability hypothesis of Proposition~1, so the symbol factorization \eqref{eq:wh} exists distributionally. The factors are however unbounded on $L^2(\R)$: $\hat C_\pm(\xi)$ diverges at $\xi=0$ and $\hat C_\pm^{-1}$ diverges as $|\xi|\to\infty$, so the projected-inverse identity of \S3.3 is an equality between unbounded operators. It holds on the dense domain fixed by the standing spectral hypothesis of \S2.1, which ensures $C^{-1}\alpha\in L^2$ and gives sense to each Marchaud composition. A bounded-operator setup is recovered by adjoining a temporary-impact term $\tfrac12\eta u_t^2$ (\S5.2): the modified symbol $M(\xi) = c_\beta|\xi|^{\beta-1}+\eta/\gamma$ has $M^{-1}\in L^\infty(\R)$, Proposition~1 applies with $C_\pm^{-1}$ bounded on $L^2$ without spectral-decay assumptions on $\alpha$, and the $\eta\to 0$ limit recovers the pure power-law formulas on the \S2.1 domain.

\emph{Finite-interval factors.} On $[0,T]$ the classical Gohberg--Krein outer construction \citep{FordeSanchezSmith2022,PorterStirling1990} gives $G_T = T\,T^*$ with
\begin{equation}\label{eq:volterra-kernel}
(T\varphi)(t) \;=\; \int_0^t k(s,t)\,\varphi(s)\,ds, \qquad k(s,t) \;=\; c_\beta^{1/2}\,(t/s)^{\nu}\,(t-s)^{\nu-1}/\Gamma(\nu),
\end{equation}
equivalently $T = c_\beta^{1/2}\, B^{-1}\, I_+^\nu\, B$ with $B(t)=t^{-\nu}$, the left-endpoint-anchored form. This outer factorization places the causal factor on the \emph{left}; the adapted projected inverse of Lemma~1 requires the causal factor on the right, $G_T = C_-\,C_+$. By the reflection $(Rf)(t)=f(T-t)$ and $R\,G_T R = G_T$, that factor is the terminal-anchored causal Volterra operator $C_+ = R\,T^*R$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:weight-conj}
C_+ \;=\; c_\beta^{1/2}\, B_T\, I_+^\nu\, B_T^{-1}, \qquad C_- = C_+^* = c_\beta^{1/2}\, B_T^{-1}\, I_-^\nu\, B_T, \qquad B_T(t) = (T-t)^{-\nu},
\end{equation}
with kernel $c_\beta^{1/2}\,\bigl((T-s)/(T-t)\bigr)^{\nu}(t-s)^{\nu-1}/\Gamma(\nu)$ on $\{s\le t\}$, so that $G_T = C_-C_+ = c_\beta\, B_T^{-1}\, I_-^\nu\, B_T^2\, I_+^\nu\, B_T^{-1}$ (verified by direct kernel integration). Both factors act boundedly between the weighted Sobolev spaces of Porter--Stirling \citep[Ex.~6.2/9.2]{PorterStirling1990} and Samko--Kilbas--Marichev \citep[\S13.5]{SamkoKilbasMarichev1993}. The terminal-anchored weight $B_T$ absorbs the loss of translation invariance at the forward endpoint and pins $C_+$ to the nest $\{P_{[0,t]}\}$ up to a diagonal phase, breaking manifest $t\leftrightarrow T{-}t$ symmetry while $G_T = C_-C_+$ preserves it.

\subsection{Bulk formula on the whole line}

Substituting $C_\pm^{-1} = c_\beta^{-1/2}\,D_\pm^\nu$ into \eqref{eq:closedform-forecast}:

\begin{corollary}[Power-law bulk formula]
For $G(t) = |t|^{-\beta}$ with $\beta\in(0,1)$, $\nu = (1-\beta)/2$, and $\alpha$ stationary adapted satisfying the standing spectral hypothesis of \S2.1,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:bulk}
u^\star_t \;=\; \gamma^{-1}\,c_\beta^{-1}\,(D_+^\nu\,\zeta)(t), \qquad \zeta_s \;=\; \bigl(D_-^\nu\,\bar\alpha(s,\cdot)\bigr)(s).
\end{equation}
\end{corollary}

The inner fractional differentiation $D_-^\nu$ acts on the forecast curve and cancels the frequency dependence of the impact operator; the outer $D_+^\nu$ colors the resulting adapted process causally. Only in the anticipative case (future signal known, no projection) would the whitened process $C_-^{-1}\alpha$ carry the flat-in-impact spectrum $c_\beta^{-1}|\xi|^{1-\beta}\,S_\alpha(\xi)$; the adapted $\zeta_s = (D_-^\nu\bar\alpha(s,\cdot))(s)$ replaces future values by forecasts and has a strictly smaller spectrum (for OU, $\zeta_s = \theta^\nu\alpha_s$ with spectrum $\theta^{2\nu}S_\alpha(\xi)$, cf.\ \eqref{eq:ou}). Without the projection the composition $D_+^\nu D_-^\nu$ would reduce (in symbol form) to the symmetric Riesz derivative $|\xi|^{1-\beta}$; adaptedness splits it into the two one-sided derivatives of order $\nu$ separated by $P_+$.

For OU $\alpha$ with mean-reversion rate $\theta$, direct Marchaud integration against the exponential forecast tail gives $(D_-^\nu\bar\alpha(t,\cdot))(t) = \theta^\nu\alpha_t$, so
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ou}
u^{\star,\,\rm OU}_t \;=\; \gamma^{-1}\,c_\beta^{-1}\,\theta^\nu\,(D_+^\nu\alpha)(t), \qquad \E\bigl[u^{\star,\,\rm OU}_t\,\bigm|\,\alpha_t\bigr] \;=\; \gamma^{-1}\,c_\beta^{-1}\,\theta^{1-\beta}\,\alpha_t,
\end{equation}
positive for every $\beta\in(0,1)$ and every $\theta>0$: the scale-free causal inverse has no zero on the imaginary axis, so no sign-flip phase transition occurs as $\theta$ varies (contrast the exponential kernel, \S5.1). The OU signal satisfies the standing spectral hypothesis of \S2.1 for $\beta>1/2$; for $\beta\le 1/2$ the OU spectrum $\sigma^2/(\theta^2+\xi^2)$ fails high-frequency integrability against $|\xi|^{2(1-\beta)+\epsilon}$, and \eqref{eq:ou} is made rigorous by adding a temporary-impact regularization (\S5.2) or by working with increments of $\alpha$.

\subsection{Boundary-deformed formula on a finite interval}

Substituting \eqref{eq:closedform} into Theorem~1:

\begin{corollary}[Power-law finite-interval formula]
For $G(t) = |t|^{-\beta}$ on $[0,T]$, the adapted optimal rate is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:bdry}
u^{\star,T}_t \;=\; \gamma^{-1}\,c_\beta^{-1}\,\bigl(B_T\, D_+^\nu\, B_T^{-1}\, P_+\, B_T^{-1}\, D_-^\nu\, B_T\,\alpha^{\eff}\bigr)(t),
\end{equation}
where $B_T(t) = (T-t)^{-\nu}$ is the terminal-anchored endpoint weight from the factors \eqref{eq:weight-conj} (the multiplications by $B_T$ commute with $P_+$ since $B_T$ is deterministic, so $B_T^{-1}P_+B_T^{-1} = P_+B_T^{-2}$) and $\alpha^{\eff} = \alpha + \sum_k\mu_k\psi_k$ is the effective signal adjoining a multiplier per position constraint --- a finite linear system for expectation constraints, and a process-valued multiplier for the pathwise liquidation constraint $X_T=0$ a.s.\ \citep{FordeSanchezSmith2022}.
\end{corollary}

The composition applies, in order: (i) terminal weight $B_T$ to the effective signal; (ii) anticausal Marchaud derivative $D_-^\nu$; (iii) reweighting by $B_T^{-1}$; (iv) adapted projection; (v) reweighting by $B_T^{-1}$; (vi) causal Marchaud derivative $D_+^\nu$; (vii) terminal weight $B_T$. The multiplications arise from substituting the reflected Gohberg--Krein factors $C_\pm$ of \eqref{eq:weight-conj} into $u^\star = \gamma^{-1}C_+^{-1}P_+C_-^{-1}\alpha^{\eff}$. Forde, S\'anchez-Betancourt and Smith \citep[Thm~2.2]{FordeSanchezSmith2022} solve the same finite-interval problem through the full inverse $G_T^{-1}=(T^*)^{-1}T^{-1}$, for which the factor order is immaterial; \eqref{eq:bdry} instead exposes the adapted projection between the two half-order factors, which requires the causal factor on the right.

Formula \eqref{eq:bdry} is a \emph{boundary-deformed fractional derivative}: the whole-line composition $D_+^\nu\,P_+\,D_-^\nu$ of \eqref{eq:bulk} is preserved as the operator core; the terminal weights $B_T, B_T^{-1}$ sandwich it, while the left endpoint enters through the truncation of the Marchaud kernels to $[0,T]$.

\subsection{Interior asymptotic and rate of convergence}

As $T\to\infty$, \eqref{eq:bdry} converges pointwise to \eqref{eq:bulk} in the interior. Let $d(t) = \min(t,T-t)$ be the distance from $t$ to the nearest boundary. Centering at the midpoint $t = T/2 + t'$, the terminal weight $B_T(T/2+t') = (T/2-t')^{-\nu}$ factors as a $T$-dependent scalar $(T/2)^{-\nu}$ times a slowly varying interior factor $(1-2t'/T)^{-\nu}$. The scalar cancels in the $B_T^{-1}\cdot B_T$ conjugations of \eqref{eq:bdry}, and the interior factor converges to 1 uniformly on compact $t'$ sets, with relative deviation $O(d(t)^{-1})$. The reflected Volterra kernel $\bigl((T-s)/(T-t)\bigr)^{\nu}(t-s)^{\nu-1}$ satisfies $\bigl((T-s)/(T-t)\bigr)^{\nu}\to 1$ in the interior, converging to the whole-line causal Riemann--Liouville kernel $(t-s)^{\nu-1}/\Gamma(\nu)$.

\begin{proposition}[Interior error bound]
For $\alpha$ bounded and effective signal $\alpha^{\eff}\in\dot H^\nu(\R)$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:interior}
\bigl|u^{\star,T}_t - u^{\star,\R}_t\bigr| \;\le\; C_1(\beta)\,\|\alpha\|_{L^\infty}\, d(t)^{-\nu} \;+\; C_2(\beta)\,\|\alpha^{\eff}\|_{\dot H^\nu}\, T^{-\nu}\, d(t)^{-\nu},
\end{equation}
with $\nu = (1-\beta)/2$ and $C_1(\beta), C_2(\beta)$ $\beta$-dependent constants.
\end{proposition}

The first term is the Marchaud truncation error from cutting the fractional-derivative tail at the boundary; it dominates the weight-conjugation deviation, which is $O(d(t)^{-1})$ and hence subdominant for $\nu<1$. The second term is the endpoint-constraint correction, entering through the S\"ohngen--Tricomi eigenfunction $\phi_1(t) = [t(T-t)]^{(\beta-1)/2}$ of the no-signal solution \citep{GatheralSchiedSlynko2012,FordeSanchezSmith2022}, whose coefficient is $O(\|\alpha^{\eff}\|_{\dot H^\nu}\,T^{-\nu})$ under the stated regularity. For empirical $\beta\in(0.2,0.6)$, $\nu\in(0.2,0.4)$, giving slow $d(t)^{-\nu}$ interior convergence --- a quantitative expression of the long memory of the impact kernel.

% =====================================================
\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Exponential kernel and the signal-speed response}

The exponential kernel $G(t) = e^{-\kappa|t|}$ is a tractable alternative to the power-law. It bounds the cost operator on $L^2(\R)$ and underlies the Obizhaeva--Wang \citeyearpar{ObizhaevaWang2013} resilience model and much of the Almgren--Chriss line of work. Working through it in the present framework does two things: it removes the domain subtleties that arise for the power-law kernel in \S4, and it makes visible which features of Theorem~1 are structural (the three-step factorization) and which are specific to the fractional case (unbounded factors, fractional-derivative discretization, absence of a sign-flip regime).

The symbol
\[
\hat C(\xi) \;=\; \frac{2\kappa}{\kappa^2+\xi^2} \;=\; \frac{\sqrt{2\kappa}}{\kappa-i\xi}\cdot\frac{\sqrt{2\kappa}}{\kappa+i\xi}
\]
splits into rational one-sided factors that are bounded on $L^2(\R)$. Their inverses are first-order differential operators,
\[
C_+^{-1} \;=\; (2\kappa)^{-1/2}(\kappa+\partial_t), \qquad C_-^{-1} \;=\; (2\kappa)^{-1/2}(\kappa-\partial_t),
\]
and substituting into Theorem~1 gives the bulk formula
\begin{equation}\label{eq:exp-bulk}
u^{\star,\,\rm exp}_t \;=\; \frac{1}{2\kappa\gamma}\,(\kappa+\partial_t)\,\zeta_t, \qquad \zeta_s \;=\; (\kappa-\partial_r)\,\bar\alpha(s,r)\bigl|_{r=s^+}.
\end{equation}
The optimal rate is a local first-order combination of the forecast curve and its time derivative --- a striking simplification of the general nonlocal recipe, made possible by the rational symbol.

For an Ornstein--Uhlenbeck signal with mean reversion $\theta$, the whitened process reduces to $\zeta_s = (\kappa+\theta)\alpha_s$, and \eqref{eq:exp-bulk} yields
\begin{equation}\label{eq:exp-ou}
u^{\star,\,\rm exp}_t \;=\; \frac{\kappa+\theta}{2\kappa\gamma}\bigl[(\kappa-\theta)\alpha_t + \sigma\dot W_t\bigr], \qquad \E\bigl[u^{\star,\,\rm exp}_t\,\bigm|\,\alpha_t\bigr] \;=\; \frac{\kappa^2-\theta^2}{2\kappa\gamma}\,\alpha_t.
\end{equation}
The white-noise term $\sigma\dot W_t$ reflects that the exponential-kernel cost does not penalize the high-frequency content of the rate, so $u^{\star,\,\rm exp}$ is a distribution-valued optimum; it is regularized to an $L^2$ process by any temporary-impact term $\tfrac12\eta u_t^2$ (\S5.2), the empirically relevant case.
The conditional mean rate flips sign at $\theta = \kappa$. When the signal mean-reverts faster than the impact tail decays ($\theta>\kappa$), the trader takes the opposite side of the signal on average: any signal-following trade would leave an impact residual longer-lived than the signal itself, so acting against the signal amortises the cost. The power-law analog \eqref{eq:ou} shows no such transition; the scale-free causal inverse has a branch point at $\xi=0$ and no zero on the imaginary axis, so no pole--zero crossing occurs as $\theta$ varies.

Both statements are instances of a kernel-general formula. Let $\alpha$ be a stationary OU signal with rate $\theta$ generating its own filtration, and define the \emph{signal-speed response} $R(\theta) := \lim_{q\downarrow 0}\E[u^\star_{t+q}\mid\alpha_t]/\alpha_t$; the innovations arriving in $(t,t+q]$ are independent of $\alpha_t$, so $R(\theta)$ is the average trade direction executed next by a trader holding the current signal value. Write
\begin{equation}\label{eq:phi}
\Phi(\theta) \;:=\; \hat C_+(i\theta) \;=\; \exp\Bigl[\frac{\theta}{2\pi}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\frac{\log\hat C(t)}{\theta^2+t^2}\,dt\Bigr] \;>\;0,
\end{equation}
the causal factor continued to the Laplace point of the signal; the Szeg\H{o}/outer representation shows $\Phi$ is real and positive because $\hat C$ is even and positive, and $\hat C_-(-i\theta) = \Phi(\theta)$ by conjugate symmetry. In the normalization of Theorem~1 the whitened process is $\zeta_s = \alpha_s/\Phi(\theta)$, consistent with $\zeta = c_\beta^{-1/2}\theta^\nu\alpha$ for the power law and, after the rescaling absorbed into \eqref{eq:exp-bulk}, with $\zeta = (\kappa+\theta)\alpha$. For symbols that are rational (finite mixtures of exponentials) or power-law,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:response}
R(\theta) \;=\; \frac{1}{\gamma\,\Phi(\theta)}\Bigl[\frac{1}{\Phi(\theta)} - 2c_1\theta\Bigr], \qquad c_1 \;=\; \lim_{|\xi|\to\infty}\bigl[(-i\xi)\,\hat C_+(\xi)\bigr]^{-1},
\end{equation}
derived in the appendix. The constant $c_1$ measures the derivative loading of the inverse causal factor: $c_1 = (-2G'(0^+))^{-1/2}$ for kernels with a kink at the origin ($\hat C(\xi)\sim -2G'(0^+)\,\xi^{-2}$ at high frequency), and $c_1 = 0$ for the power-law cusp. The exponential kernel has $\Phi = \sqrt{2\kappa}/(\kappa+\theta)$ and $c_1 = (2\kappa)^{-1/2}$, and \eqref{eq:response} returns $(\kappa^2-\theta^2)/2\kappa\gamma$; the power-law kernel has $\Phi(\theta)^2 = c_\beta\theta^{\beta-1} = \hat C(\theta)$, and \eqref{eq:response} returns $\theta^{1-\beta}/\gamma c_\beta$ --- the cost symbol inverted at real frequency equal to the signal speed.

Formula \eqref{eq:response} settles when contrarian trading is optimal: $\operatorname{sign} R(\theta) = \operatorname{sign}\bigl(1 - 2c_1\theta\,\Phi(\theta)\bigr)$. For kink kernels $2c_1\theta\,\Phi(\theta)\to 2$ as $\theta\to\infty$, so every sufficiently fast signal is traded against; for the power-law cusp $R(\theta)>0$ at every signal speed. The two-exponential mixture $G(t) = w_1e^{-\kappa_1|t|}+w_2e^{-\kappa_2|t|}$ has $\hat C_+(\xi) = \sqrt{2W}\,(\mu-i\xi)/[(\kappa_1-i\xi)(\kappa_2-i\xi)]$ with $W = w_1\kappa_1+w_2\kappa_2$ and $\mu^2 = (w_1\kappa_1\kappa_2^2+w_2\kappa_2\kappa_1^2)/W$, giving
\begin{equation}\label{eq:mixture}
R(\theta) \;=\; \frac{(\kappa_1+\theta)(\kappa_2+\theta)\,\bigl[(\kappa_1+\theta)(\kappa_2+\theta) - 2\theta(\mu+\theta)\bigr]}{2\gamma\,W\,(\mu+\theta)^2},
\end{equation}
with exactly one sign change, at $\theta^* = \tfrac12\bigl[(\kappa_1+\kappa_2-2\mu) + \sqrt{(\kappa_1+\kappa_2-2\mu)^2+4\kappa_1\kappa_2}\bigr]$: the bracket in \eqref{eq:mixture} is a downward parabola in $\theta$, positive at $\theta=0$. Setting $\kappa_1=\kappa_2=\kappa$ recovers $\theta^*=\kappa$. Discretized adapted optima reproduce \eqref{eq:response}--\eqref{eq:mixture} to discretization accuracy. The regression of $u^\star_t$ on the contemporaneous $\alpha_t$ instead equals $1/\gamma\Phi(\theta)^2 > 0$ for every kernel: the contrarian term $-2c_1\theta$ is carried by the innovation atom in the optimal rate --- the white-noise component of \eqref{eq:exp-ou} --- which is present exactly when singular trading rates have finite cost, i.e.\ when $G$ is bounded at the origin.

\subsection{Temporary impact}

Adding a temporary-impact term $\tfrac12\eta u_t^2$ modifies the FOC symbol to $M(\xi) = c_\beta|\xi|^{\beta-1}+\eta/\gamma$. The added constant provides high-frequency coercivity that the pure power-law symbol lacks, so $u^\star\in L^2$ without spectral-decay assumptions on $\alpha$. Wiener--Hopf factorization of $M$ gives modified one-sided factors; the crossover frequency $\xi_* = (\gamma c_\beta/\eta)^{1/(1-\beta)}$ separates a long-memory fractional regime ($|\xi|\ll\xi_*$) from a myopic signal-following regime ($|\xi|\gg\xi_*$) in which $u^\star\approx\alpha/\eta$. The $\eta\to 0$ limit is singular but recovers \eqref{eq:bulk} under the spectral-decay hypothesis of \S2.1.

\subsection{Multi-asset extension}

Empirical cross-impact between correlated assets --- trades in one name moving others in the same principal-component sector --- motivates a portfolio version of Theorem~1. For a cross-impact kernel $\mathbf{K}(t) = |t|^{-\beta}\mathbf{A}$ with $\mathbf{A} = Q\Lambda Q^\top$ symmetric positive-definite, Theorem~1 diagonalizes in the eigenbasis of $\mathbf{A}$: the scalar fractional-derivative rule \eqref{eq:bulk} applies independently to each principal-component alpha with eigenvalue prefactor $\Lambda_{ii}^{-1}$. The symmetric case covers the leading positive-definite parameterization; non-symmetric cross-impact requires a matrix Wiener--Hopf factorization of $\mathbf{A}(\xi)$, which exists under the same log-integrability hypothesis but may carry non-zero partial indices, in which case the factor is not a simple fractional integral and the scalar reduction fails \citep{GohbergKrein1970}.

\subsection{Numerical implementation}

Fractional derivatives discretize to Toeplitz matrices. On a uniform grid of $N$ points, $D_\pm^\nu$ is a lower- or upper-triangular Toeplitz whose entries are generalized binomial coefficients from the expansion of $(1-z)^\nu$. Evaluating \eqref{eq:bulk} over the whole grid costs $O(N\log N)$ via FFT, compared with $O(N^2)$ Nystr\"om inversion of the Fredholm equation. The finite-interval formula \eqref{eq:bdry} adds diagonal multiplications by $B_T$ and $B_T^{-1}$, preserving the $O(N\log N)$ cost.

\subsection{Role of the forecast curve: innovations filter and value}

Theorem~1 makes explicit that the optimal rate at time $t$ depends on the \emph{entire} forecast curve $\bar\alpha(t,\cdot)$. When $\alpha$ is a stationary, purely non-deterministic Gaussian process generating its own filtration, the dependence collapses to a single transfer function. Let $S_\alpha = |\varphi_+|^2$ with $\varphi_+$ the outer spectral factor (Szeg\H{o} condition $\int\log S_\alpha(\xi)/(1+\xi^2)\,d\xi>-\infty$), so that $\alpha = \varphi_+(D)\dot W$ is a causal moving average of its innovations \citep{Wiener1949}, and let $\Pi_+$ denote the Riesz projection: truncation of a moving-average kernel to non-negative lags. Conditional expectation of a stationary linear functional of $W$ truncates its kernel at lag zero, so the adapted projection of Theorem~1 becomes $\Pi_+$ and
\begin{equation}\label{eq:innov}
u^\star \;=\; \hat g(D)\,\dot W, \qquad \hat g \;=\; \gamma^{-1}\,\hat C_+^{-1}\,\Pi_+\!\bigl[\hat C_-^{-1}\varphi_+\bigr],
\end{equation}
with $\hat g\in L^2$ under the standing spectral hypothesis (appendix). The optimal rate is a stationary causal filter of the signal innovations --- the Wiener--Kolmogorov form of the three-step recipe. Its spectrum is $S_{u^\star} = |\hat g|^2$; for the power-law kernel $S_{u^\star}$ vanishes like $|\xi|^{1-\beta}$ at low frequency whenever $S_\alpha(0)<\infty$, so cost-optimal flow is anti-persistent at long horizons.

\emph{Value of a forecast.} Stationarity makes the value \emph{rate} (expected P\&L per unit time) the relevant quantity, $v(u) = \E[u_t\alpha_t] - \tfrac{\gamma}{2}\E[u_t(Cu)_t]$; at the optimum $v = \tfrac12\E[u^\star_t\alpha_t]$, and \eqref{eq:innov} evaluates it:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:value}
v_{\rm ad} \;=\; \frac{1}{4\pi\gamma}\,\bigl\|\Pi_+\bigl(\hat C_-^{-1}\varphi_+\bigr)\bigr\|_{L^2}^2, \qquad v_{\rm ant} - v_{\rm ad} \;=\; \frac{1}{4\pi\gamma}\,\bigl\|\Pi_-\bigl(\hat C_-^{-1}\varphi_+\bigr)\bigr\|_{L^2}^2,
\end{equation}
where $v_{\rm ant} = \frac{1}{4\pi\gamma}\int S_\alpha(\xi)/\hat C(\xi)\,d\xi$ is the value rate of the perfectly-anticipating optimum $\gamma^{-1}C^{-1}\alpha$ and $\Pi_- = I-\Pi_+$. The half-whitened spectral factor $\hat C_-^{-1}\varphi_+$ splits into a causal part, which prices the adapted problem, and an anticausal remainder, whose energy is the exact cost of causality. For the power-law kernel and an OU signal, $\Pi_+((i\xi)^\nu\varphi_+) = \theta^\nu\varphi_+$ (pole cancellation, appendix), giving
\[
v_{\rm ad} \;=\; \frac{\sigma^2\,\theta^{-\beta}}{4\gamma c_\beta}, \qquad \frac{v_{\rm ad}}{v_{\rm ant}} \;=\; \sin(\pi\beta/2):
\]
adaptedness costs nothing in the short-memory limit $\beta\to1$ and destroys all value in the permanent-impact limit $\beta\to0$. The weighting of the signal spectrum by $|\xi|^{1-\beta}$ in $v_{\rm ant}$, and by the causal-energy functional in $v_{\rm ad}$, differs from an $R^2$ of the signal against future returns: two signals with identical $R^2$ can differ arbitrarily in tradeable value once their timescales differ, and \eqref{eq:value} quantifies what makes one forecast better than another for a given impact kernel.

\emph{Forecast horizon.} The Marchaud representation of $D_-^\nu$ averages forecast increments $\bar\alpha(s,r) - \bar\alpha(s,s)$ over all future lags $r > s$ with weight $(r-s)^{-1-\nu}$. Forecasts are required over all horizons; horizon truncation gives an error decaying like $r_{\max}^{-\nu}$ in the maximum forecast horizon.

\subsection{Joint gain--risk--cost formulation}

The joint gain--risk--cost problem inherits the same operator structure once the two frictions share a coordinate. Cost acts on the trading rate $u$; a mean--variance holding penalty $\tfrac{\lambda}{2}\E\int x_t^\top\Sigma x_t\,dt$ acts on the position $x_t = \int_{-\infty}^t u_s\,ds$, which in rate coordinates is the operator with Fourier symbol $\lambda\Sigma/\xi^2$. Adding it to \eqref{eq:gaincost} replaces $C$ by the symbol $\gamma\hat C(\xi) + \lambda\Sigma/\xi^2$ --- still a positive Toeplitz operator, admitting Wiener--Hopf and Gohberg--Krein factorization, to which Lemma~1 applies without modification. The pure trading limit ($\lambda\to 0$) and the pure holding limit ($\gamma\to 0$) are the two boundaries of this joint factorization.

% =====================================================
\section{Concluding Remarks}

This paper establishes a closed-form solution to the gain--cost trading problem under a general symmetric positive-definite propagator kernel. The construction rests on the causal--anticausal factorization of the cost operator --- Wiener--Hopf on the whole line, Gohberg--Krein on a finite interval, both realized as instances of the Arveson outer factorization for positive operators on a continuously nested Hilbert space. The adapted optimal rate is expressed as the composition of the causal square-root inverse of the cost operator with the adapted projection of the anticausal square-root inverse applied to the forecast curve, an expression whose form depends on the kernel only through its factorization.

For the power-law kernel, the whole-line factors are the Liouville (Weyl) fractional integrals of order $\nu = (1-\beta)/2$, and the closed form expresses the optimal rate as a composition of an anticausal and a causal Marchaud fractional derivative of order $\nu$, separated by the adapted projection $P_+$. Without $P_+$ the composition would reduce to the symmetric Riesz derivative of order $1-\beta$; the projection preserves the two half-order pieces and encodes the causality constraint. On a finite horizon the fractional integrals acquire terminal-anchored endpoint-weight conjugations induced by the reflected Gohberg--Krein factorization, and the optimal rate takes the form of a boundary-deformed fractional derivative that converges to the whole-line formula in the interior at rate $O(d(t)^{-\nu})$.

% =====================================================
\appendix
\section{Appendix}

\paragraph{Proof of Lemma 1 (whole-line case).} With $C_\pm = c_\beta^{1/2} I_\pm^\nu$ on $L^2(\R)$, $C_\pm$ are Hilbert-space isomorphisms between $\Ldot{-\nu}$ and $L^2$ (respectively $L^2$ and $\Ldot{\nu}$) by Plancherel and the symbol $c_\beta^{1/2}|\xi|^{(\beta-1)/2}$. Their adjointness $C_+^* = C_-$ follows from the kernel-flip $(t-s)^{\nu-1}\mathbf{1}_{s\le t}\mapsto(s-t)^{\nu-1}\mathbf{1}_{t\le s}$. On $L^2_{\adap}\cap\Ldot{-\nu}$ the composition $P_+ C P_+$ is bounded, symmetric, and strictly positive (from $\hat C > 0$ on $\xi\ne 0$), hence boundedly invertible on its range. Causality of $C_+$ implies $P_+^\perp C_+ P_+ = 0$, so $C_+$ preserves $L^2_{\adap}$ and $C_+^{-1}$ commutes with $P_+$ on adapted inputs. The adjoint identity $P_+ C_- P_+^\perp = 0$ follows. Taking adjoints of $C_+^{-1}\colon L^2 \to \Ldot{-\nu}$ gives $P_+ C_-^{-1} P_+^\perp = 0$ as well, so $C_-^{-1}$ preserves ${L^2_{\adap}}^\perp$. For adapted $u$: $P_+ C P_+ u = P_+ C_- C_+ u$; applying $C_+^{-1} P_+ C_-^{-1}$ returns $u$ via $P_+ C_-^{-1} P_+ C_-$ acting as $P_+$ on the adapted vector $C_+ u$.

\paragraph{Proof of Lemma 1 (finite-interval case).} Take $C_+ = RT^*R$ the reflected causal Gohberg--Krein factor of Proposition~2 and $C_- = C_+^* = RTR$, so that $C_-C_+ = R(TT^*)R = RG_TR = G_T$. Volterra causality of $C_+$ (kernel on $\{s\le t\}\cap[0,T]^2$) gives $P_+^\perp C_+ P_+ = 0$ and $P_+ C_- P_+^\perp = 0$; the composition argument of the whole-line case then applies verbatim.

\paragraph{Existence of the outer factor.} The Wiener--Hopf factorization on $\R$ under the log-integrability hypothesis is classical \citep{WienerHopf1931,Krein1962}. The finite-interval outer factorization $G_T = TT^*$ is Arveson's theorem for positive operators on a continuous nest \citep[Thm~4.4.2]{Arveson1975}; an equivalent constructive proof for symmetric weakly-singular integral kernels via spectral square root is in \citep[Ex.~6.2/9.2]{PorterStirling1990}; the explicit left-anchored factor \eqref{eq:volterra-kernel} for the power-law kernel is derived in \citep[pp.~590--591]{FordeSanchezSmith2022}, and the reflected causal factor \eqref{eq:weight-conj} required here follows from it by the reflection $R$.

\paragraph{Proof of Corollary 1.} Define the candidate $u^{\rm cand}_t := \gamma^{-1}c_\beta^{-1}(D_+^\nu\zeta)(t)$. Adaptedness: $D_+^\nu$ at time $t$ depends only on $\{\zeta_s\}_{s\le t}$, and each $\zeta_s \in \F_s\subset\F_t$ by \eqref{eq:proj-cma} and $\F_s$-measurability of the forecast curve. FOC verification proceeds in three steps.

\emph{Step (a).} Conditional Fubini \citep[Thm~14.16]{Klenke2014} applied to the Marchaud representation gives $\E_t[(D_+^\nu\zeta)(v)] = (D_+^\nu \hat\zeta_t)(v)$ with $\hat\zeta_t(s) := \E_t[\zeta_s]$.

\emph{Step (b).} For $s \le t$: $\zeta_s \in \F_s\subset\F_t$, so $\hat\zeta_t(s) = \zeta_s$. For $s > t$: by tower and conditional Fubini applied at conditioning time $t$, $\hat\zeta_t(s) = (D_-^\nu\bar\alpha(t,\cdot))(s)$.

\emph{Step (c).} The symbol identity $\hat C(\xi)(-i\xi)^\nu = c_\beta(i\xi)^{-\nu}$ yields the operator identity $C\,D_+^\nu = c_\beta I_-^\nu$ on $L^2(\R)$. Substituting:
\[
\gamma\,\E_t\!\bigl[(Cu^{\rm cand})(t)\bigr] = (I_-^\nu\hat\zeta_t)(t) = (I_-^\nu D_-^\nu\bar\alpha(t,\cdot))(t) = \bar\alpha(t,t) = \alpha_t,
\]
using $I_-^\nu D_-^\nu = \mathrm{id}$ on $H^\nu(\R)$ \citep[\S5.3 Thm 5.3]{SamkoKilbasMarichev1993}, applied to the future-half-line piece of the forecast curve, which is in $H^\nu$ pathwise under the standing spectral hypothesis, and the fact that $I_-^\nu$ at $t$ samples only $\hat\zeta_t(s)$ for $s\ge t$, where Step (b) gives the closed form.

\emph{Uniqueness.} $\hat C(\xi) = c_\beta|\xi|^{\beta-1}>0$ on $\xi\ne 0$, so the quadratic penalty in \eqref{eq:gaincost} is strictly convex on $L^2_{\adap}$ and the adapted FOC has a unique solution.

\emph{Admissibility.} The spectral hypothesis ensures $|\xi|^{1-\beta}\widehat{\bar\alpha}(\xi)\in L^2$ pathwise, so $u^\star\in L^2_{\adap}$ by Plancherel.\qed

\paragraph{Proof of Corollary 2.} The anticausal Marchaud derivative $D_-^\nu$ in \eqref{eq:bdry} acts on $\alpha^{\eff}$ extended by zero outside $[0,T]$, the natural convention for the finite-interval Volterra calculus \citep[\S13.5]{SamkoKilbasMarichev1993}. Substitute the reflected factors \eqref{eq:weight-conj} into \eqref{eq:closedform}: $u^{\star,T} = \gamma^{-1}\,C_+^{-1}\,P_+\,C_-^{-1}\,\alpha^{\eff}$ with $C_+^{-1} = c_\beta^{-1/2}\, B_T D_+^\nu B_T^{-1}$ and $C_-^{-1} = c_\beta^{-1/2}\, B_T^{-1} D_-^\nu B_T$ (inverting \eqref{eq:weight-conj} using $(I_\pm^\nu)^{-1} = D_\pm^\nu$). Collecting the two $c_\beta^{-1/2}$ factors into $c_\beta^{-1}$ gives \eqref{eq:bdry}. $C_+^{-1}$ is a Volterra operator between the appropriate weighted Sobolev spaces \citep[\S13.5]{SamkoKilbasMarichev1993}, not on $L^2$ directly. Adaptedness follows from causality of $C_+^{-1}$ (Volterra inverse of Volterra is Volterra).\qed

\paragraph{Proof of Proposition 3.} The Marchaud tail bound: $|(D_-^\nu f)(s) - (D_-^{\nu,[0,T]}f)(s)| \le \tfrac{2\|f\|_\infty}{\Gamma(1-\nu)}(T-s)^{-\nu}$ from
\[
(D_-^\nu f)(s) = \tfrac{\nu}{\Gamma(1-\nu)}\!\int_0^\infty \tfrac{f(s)-f(s+r)}{r^{1+\nu}}\,dr,
\]
truncated at $r = T-s$. Symmetrically the outer $D_+^\nu$ contributes a tail bounded by $t^{-\nu}$. The dominant interior truncation is on the closer boundary, giving the first term of \eqref{eq:interior}. The terminal-weight conjugation contributes a further error: on the Marchaud support the ratio $B_T(t)/B_T(s) = \bigl((T-s)/(T-t)\bigr)^{\nu} = 1 + O((t-s)/(T-t))$, so its deviation from the whole-line unweighted operator is $O(d(t)^{-1})$, subdominant to $d(t)^{-\nu}$ for $\nu<1$. The endpoint-constraint correction enters through the S\"ohngen--Tricomi eigenfunction $\phi_1(t) = [t(T-t)]^{(\beta-1)/2}$ of the no-signal solution \citep{GatheralSchiedSlynko2012,FordeSanchezSmith2022}, which satisfies $|\phi_1(t)|\le d(t)^{-\nu}(T/2)^{-\nu}$; under $\alpha^{\eff}\in\dot H^\nu$ its coefficient is $O(\|\alpha^{\eff}\|_{\dot H^\nu}\,T^{-\nu})$, giving the second term of \eqref{eq:interior}.\qed

\paragraph{Derivation of the innovations form and value \eqref{eq:innov}--\eqref{eq:value}.} $C_-^{-1}\alpha$ is the stationary filter of $W$ with symbol $h := \hat C_-^{-1}\varphi_+$. For fixed $s$, $\E_s$ annihilates the innovations after $s$, so $(P_+C_-^{-1}\alpha)_s$ is the filter with kernel truncated to non-negative lags, symbol $\Pi_+h$; composing with $C_+^{-1}$ gives \eqref{eq:innov}. Membership: $|\xi|^\nu h = c_\beta^{-1/2}|\xi|^{2\nu}\varphi_+\in L^2$ by the standing hypothesis, so $h\in H^\nu(\R)$; the half-line indicator is a bounded pointwise multiplier on $H^\nu$ for $\nu<1/2$ \citep[Ch.~1]{LionsMagenes1972}, so $\Pi_+h\in H^\nu$ and $\hat g = \gamma^{-1}\hat C_+^{-1}\Pi_+h\in L^2$. Value: the FOC \eqref{eq:foc} and adaptedness of $u^\star$ give $\E[u^\star_t\alpha_t] = \gamma\,\E[u^\star_t(Cu^\star)_t]$, hence $v = \tfrac12\E[u^\star_t\alpha_t]$. By the It\^o isometry and Plancherel, $\E[u^\star_t\alpha_t] = \tfrac{1}{2\pi}\int\hat g\,\overline{\varphi_+}\,d\xi$; since $\overline{\varphi_+} = \overline{\hat C_-\,h} = \hat C_+\overline{h}$ on the real axis, the integrand is $\gamma^{-1}(\Pi_+h)\,\overline{h}$, and self-adjointness of $\Pi_+$ gives $\gamma^{-1}\|\Pi_+h\|^2/2\pi$, the first formula in \eqref{eq:value}. The anticipative optimum replaces $\Pi_+h$ by $h$; the gap is $\|\Pi_-h\|^2$ by Pythagoras. OU pole cancellation: with $\varphi_+ = \sigma/(\theta-i\xi)$,
\[
h - \frac{\varphi_+}{\hat C_-(-i\theta)} \;=\; \Bigl[\frac{1}{\hat C_-(\xi)} - \frac{1}{\hat C_-(-i\theta)}\Bigr]\frac{\sigma}{\theta-i\xi}
\]
is square-integrable and analytic in the lower half-plane (the pole at $\xi = -i\theta$ is removed and $1/\hat C_-$ is analytic there), hence anticausal; so $\Pi_+h = \varphi_+/\hat C_-(-i\theta) = \varphi_+/\Phi(\theta)$. For the power-law kernel $\Phi(\theta) = c_\beta^{1/2}\theta^{-\nu}$, and $\|\varphi_+\|^2 = \pi\sigma^2/\theta$, $\|h\|^2 = c_\beta^{-1}\sigma^2\theta^{2\nu-1}\,\pi/\cos(\pi\nu)$ give the displayed $v_{\rm ad}$ and the ratio $\cos(\pi\nu) = \sin(\pi\beta/2)$.

\paragraph{Derivation of the response formula \eqref{eq:response}.} By the pole cancellation above, $\zeta = \alpha/\Phi(\theta)$ and $\hat g(\xi) = \gamma^{-1}\sigma/[\Phi(\theta)\,\hat C_+(\xi)\,(\theta-i\xi)]$. For $q>0$, $\mathrm{Cov}(u^\star_{t+q},\alpha_t) = \sigma\int_0^\infty g(q+r)\,e^{-\theta r}\,dr$ with $g$ the time-domain kernel of $\hat g$. For rational symbols $\hat g(\xi)\to a_0 := \gamma^{-1}\sigma c_1/\Phi(\theta)$ as $|\xi|\to\infty$, so $g = a_0\delta + g_{\rm reg}$ with $g_{\rm reg}$ integrable against $e^{-\theta\cdot}$; the atom at lag $0$ leaves the integral for every $q>0$, and dominated convergence gives $\mathrm{Cov}\to\sigma[(\mathcal Lg)(\theta) - a_0]$ as $q\downarrow0$, where $(\mathcal Lg)(\theta) = \hat g(i\theta) = \gamma^{-1}\sigma/[2\theta\,\Phi(\theta)^2]$. Dividing by $\mathrm{Var}(\alpha_t) = \sigma^2/2\theta$ yields \eqref{eq:response}. For the power-law class $\hat g(\xi) = O(|\xi|^{\nu-1})\to0$, so $a_0 = c_1 = 0$ and $R = 1/\gamma\Phi(\theta)^2$. The same limit computes the position-increment response $\lim_{\Delta\downarrow0}\E[x_{t+\Delta}-x_t\mid\alpha_t]/\Delta\alpha_t$.\qed

